{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019W2293Vmhb3VnLja1gxkzE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Rust translation of c_src/ (MIT Lincoln Laboratory `driver` library).\n2\t//\n3\t// Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n4\t//     T driver\n5\t//\n6\t// The C implementation is:\n7\t//     void driver(const char *s1, const char *s2) {\n8\t//         printf(\"%zu\\n\", strcspn(s1, s2));\n9\t//     }\n10\t\n11\tuse std::ffi::c_char;\n12\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n13\t\n14\textern \"C\" {\n15\t    // Use the platform's printf so that formatting *and* stdio buffering\n16\t    // semantics are byte-for-byte identical to the C library's output.\n17\t    fn printf(fmt: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n18\t}\n19\t\n20\t/// Faithful reimplementation of C's `strcspn`.\n21\t///\n22\t/// Returns the length of the initial segment of `s1` consisting of characters\n23\t/// that do not appear in `s2`.  The terminating NUL of `s2` is not considered\n24\t/// part of the reject set, so an empty `s2` yields `strlen(s1)`.\n25\t///\n26\t/// # Argument-access order (observable, and therefore part of the contract)\n27\t///\n28\t/// The C library's `strcspn` builds a lookup table from the reject set *before*\n29\t/// it looks at `s1`, so `s2` is scanned **in full, unconditionally** — even when\n30\t/// `s1` is empty and the answer is already known to be 0. That ordering is\n31\t/// observable: `strcspn(\"\", NULL)` faults in C rather than returning 0. This\n32\t/// implementation reproduces it by scanning `s2` first for exactly the same\n33\t/// number of bytes (up to and including its NUL, never past it) and only then\n34\t/// walking `s1`.\n35\t///\n36\t/// # Safety\n37\t/// `s1` and `s2` must be valid NUL-terminated C strings (the C code has the\n38\t/// same requirement and would likewise fault on NULL).\n39\tunsafe fn strcspn(s1: *const c_char, s2: *const c_char) -> usize {\n40\t    // Pass 1: consume the whole reject set, exactly as the C library does\n41\t    // before it touches `s1`. Reads bytes s2[0..=n] where s2[n] is the NUL.\n42\t    let mut table = [false; 256];\n43\t    let mut q = s2;\n44\t    loop {\n45\t        let d = *q as u8;\n46\t        if d == 0 {\n47\t            break;\n48\t        }\n49\t        table[d as usize] = true;\n50\t        q = q.add(1);\n51\t    }\n52\t\n53\t    // Pass 2: walk `s1`, stopping at its NUL or at the first rejected byte.\n54\t    // Reads no byte of `s1` beyond the one that stops the scan.\n55\t    let mut p = s1;\n56\t    loop {\n57\t        let c = *p as u8;\n58\t        if c == 0 || table[c as usize] {\n59\t            break;\n60\t        }\n61\t        p = p.add(1);\n62\t    }\n63\t    p.offset_from(s1) as usize\n64\t}\n65\t\n66\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n67\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(s1: *const c_char, s2: *const c_char) {\n68\t    printf(b\"%zu\\n\\0\".as_ptr() as *const c_char, strcspn(s1, s2));\n69\t}\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d137c05b-992d-4c98-844f-1d3b53a3e143","uuid":"83d9d309-c853-4d17-9cf9-a1b3b2e4cbfc","timestamp":"2026-08-28T15:14:39.075Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-yy43rr/translation/src/lib.rs","content":"// Rust translation of c_src/ (MIT Lincoln Laboratory `driver` library).\n//\n// Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n//     T driver\n//\n// The C implementation is:\n//     void driver(const char *s1, const char *s2) {\n//         printf(\"%zu\\n\", strcspn(s1, s2));\n//     }\n\nuse std::ffi::c_char;\nuse std::ffi::c_int;\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    // Use the platform's printf so that formatting *and* stdio buffering\n    // semantics are byte-for-byte identical to the C library's output.\n    fn printf(fmt: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n}\n\n/// Faithful reimplementation of C's `strcspn`.\n///\n/// Returns the length of the initial segment of `s1` consisting of characters\n/// that do not appear in `s2`.  The terminating NUL of `s2` is not considered\n/// part of the reject set, so an empty `s2` yields `strlen(s1)`.\n///\n/// # Argument-access order (observable, and therefore part of the contract)\n///\n/// The C library's `strcspn` builds a lookup table from the reject set *before*\n/// it looks at `s1`, so `s2` is scanned **in full, unconditionally** — even when\n/// `s1` is empty and the answer is already known to be 0. That ordering is\n/// observable: `strcspn(\"\", NULL)` faults in C rather than returning 0. This\n/// implementation reproduces it by scanning `s2` first for exactly the same\n/// number of bytes (up to and including its NUL, never past it) and only then\n/// walking `s1`.\n///\n/// # Safety\n/// `s1` and `s2` must be valid NUL-terminated C strings (the C code has the\n/// same requirement and would likewise fault on NULL).\nunsafe fn strcspn(s1: *const c_char, s2: *const c_char) -> usize {\n    // Pass 1: consume the whole reject set, exactly as the C library does\n    // before it touches `s1`. Reads bytes s2[0..=n] where s2[n] is the NUL.\n    let mut table = [false; 256];\n    let mut q = s2;\n    loop {\n        let d = *q as u8;\n        if d == 0 {\n            break;\n        }\n        table[d as usize] = true;\n        q = q.add(1);\n    }\n\n    // Pass 2: walk `s1`, stopping at its NUL or at the first rejected byte.\n    // Reads no byte of `s1` beyond the one that stops the scan.\n    let mut p = s1;\n    loop {\n        let c = *p as u8;\n        if c == 0 || table[c as usize] {\n            break;\n        }\n        p = p.add(1);\n    }\n    p.offset_from(s1) as usize\n}\n\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(s1: *const c_char, s2: *const c_char) {\n    printf(b\"%zu\\n\\0\".as_ptr() as *const c_char, strcspn(s1, s2));\n}\n","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"totalLines":70}}}
