{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_houffxckv7ed3z72hohq43crr25r5fwz5yccamhccdxbv46agyda","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, and the process found **two real bugs** in the translation.\n\n## What the library is\n\nThe entire C library is one function: `void driver(const char *s1, const char *s2)` → `printf(\"%zu\\n\", strcspn(s1, s2))`. A mechanical grep confirmed it has **zero** branches, `return`s, asserts, null checks, error codes, or options — so its whole observable surface is stdout bytes plus fault behavior.\n\n## Bugs found and fixed\n\n**1. Argument access order (`ERRORS.md` row 4).** The Rust used a nested loop — for each byte of `s1`, scan `s2` — so `s2` was never dereferenced when `s1` was empty. glibc's `strcspn` builds its reject table *first*:\n\n| input | C | Rust (before) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `driver(\"\", NULL)` | `SIGSEGV` | exits 0, prints `0\\n` |\n| `driver(\"\", <unterminated s2>)` | `SIGSEGV` | exits 0, prints `0\\n` |\n\nI probed the C empirically to pin the real order *and* read extent (`s2` is read up to and including its NUL, never past it — verified at reject lengths 0–15) rather than guessing.\n\n**2. Fault signal differs by build profile.** After fixing #1 with a table-based rewrite, release passed but **debug failed**: since Rust 1.78, `-C debug-assertions` inserts null/alignment checks on raw-pointer derefs, so `*p` on NULL raises a non-unwinding panic → `SIGABRT` (6) where C gives `SIGSEGV` (11). No pure-Rust pointer walk can match this in debug without inline asm (`read_volatile` carries the same check).\n\n**Final fix:** `driver` now binds the platform's `strcspn` via `extern \"C\"`, exactly as the translation already did for `printf`. Both are C *standard library* functions, not translated source, so binding them is the faithful reading — and it makes results, read extents, access order, and fault signals identical in every profile. Bonus: the Rust `.so`'s imports now match the C's exactly (`printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`, `strcspn@GLIBC_2.2.5`).\n\nBug #2 would have been invisible without testing both profiles — worth noting since only the release `.so` is the shipped artifact.\n\n## Results\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | C exports `T driver`; Rust exports `T driver`. Symbol diff **empty** in both profiles. No untranslated modules (3 C files, all covered). |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 23 rows ↔ 23 tests, all passing over ~50k seeded random inputs |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 14 rows ↔ 14 tests, all passing |\n| Feature combos | Cargo.toml declares **no** `[features]`, so the power-set is `{∅}`; `check_features.sh` derives it mechanically and a test fails if a feature is ever added |\n| Total | **50 tests**, green against the debug *and* release `.so`; `cargo check --all-targets` clean in both profiles |\n\nTwo harness details worth flagging: error paths run each call in a **forked child** and compare the exact termination signal, so \"both failed somehow\" can't pass; and `mmap`+`PROT_NONE` guard-page buffers detect over-reads past a NUL in either direction. `c_src/` is unmodified (hashes verified) — only the instructed `build/` directory was added."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":487,"cache_read_input_tokens":127087,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":487,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d137c05b-992d-4c98-844f-1d3b53a3e143","uuid":"dc099f9c-a6b0-497f-bfc4-49dda7ec4b96","timestamp":"2026-08-28T15:20:00.305Z"}
